@GregHBurnham - Ideal metric would be something like this: how much does it cost to produce software (e.g. computer game) that a consumer would value at $100? But pretty hard to operationalize that. Also peoples' willingness-to-pay depends on the alternatives and the whole market moves.

- algorithmic efficiency metrics are good -- e.g. maybe geekbench or similar on fixed hardware?

- rate of closing issues would be useful, but hard to keep constant the inflow of issues. Maybe closing historical pre-AI-era issues/bugs?

2102214639258104223.text:
@sandersted OK great. 

I think I would count algorithmic efficiency and vulnerability discovery as *sufficient statistics*, meaning that just observing those numbers tells you how much value you're getting. If you've discovered a high-severity zero-day, or you've got a 10% efficiency improvement, then (in the simplified case) you don't need other know more about the nature of the output.

This isn't true for lines-of-code, or pull-requests, or time-spent, or working-papers. In all those cases you could massively increase the quantity with essentially zero real value.

